    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d813">

                <pb facs="1097" n="1087" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1087"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">896.24/10–1146: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Manila</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steintorf</hi>)<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d813-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-10-11T16:00:00-05:00">October 11, 1946—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1450. From Connolly<anchor n="20" xml:id="d813-f2"/> and Starr<anchor n="21"
                        xml:id="d813-f3"/> to Vogelback. Re Shanghai tel No. 89, October 7.<anchor
                        n="22" xml:id="d813-f4"/> It is our view that August 30th agreement
                    supersedes all previous tentative proposals or agreements and is the controlling
                        <pb facs="1098" n="1088" xml:id="pg1088"/> document. Desired is your opinion
                    and proposed action in the circumstances. What significance need be given to
                    policy outlined your No. 3719, Vogelback to McCabe,<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d813-f5"/> 2 July?<anchor n="24" xml:id="d813-f6"/> Was this
                    proposed policy presented to and orally agreed upon by Chinese officials and if
                    so was it in your judgement superseded by August 30th agreement. [Connolly and
                    Starr.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d813-f1" n="19" type="source" xml:id="d813-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai as telegram No. 1816.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d813-f2" n="20" xml:id="d813-f2-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Donald H. Connolly, Foreign Liquidation Commissioner.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d813-f3" n="21" xml:id="d813-f3-content">
                <p>Col. Edward Starr, Jr., Special Assistant to the Foreign Liquidation
                    Commissioner.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d813-f4" n="22" xml:id="d813-f4-content">
                <p>This telegram repeated No. 1929 bis, October 7, 3 p.m., from the Consul General
                    at Shanghai, <ref target="#pg1084">p. 1084</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d813-f5" n="23" xml:id="d813-f5-content">
                <p>Thomas B. McCabe, then Foreign Liquidation Commissioner and Special Assistant to
                    the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d813-f6" n="24" xml:id="d813-f6-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg1043">p. 1043</ref>. The
                    answer to this query was given in a teletype conversation between the OFLC
                    Washington and Manila Offices on October 11. The Manila Office reported that “my
                    radio of July second regarding earmarking of sales was a decision taken at
                    meeting of field commissioners on June twenty ninth in which it was specifically
                    understood and agreed that this decision would not be a commitment to China but
                    would be used at the time in final negotiations as a spur to quick action and as
                    trading material.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
